  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.3 × loss. Left to right (x) is 4 times steeper, so steps overshoot there first. Above a learning rate of 0.5 (2/4), x swings grow. Below it they shrink on average.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise has spread 2·√((64−b)/(63b)) per axis for batch size b: largest at 1, exactly 0 at 64 (all points).",
    "Arrows show steps at 2× their true length, one scale for both. Mini-batch step is where the ball moves next. Full-data step is −learning rate × true slope. The noise is fixed by a seed, so Reset replays the same values.",
    "A run is finished after 40 steps. It is diverged once the loss passes 10, the highest loss on the drawn bowl. Points past the rim are drawn at the rim, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average. Small batches can raise it on a single step."
  ]
}